*The governance extension interface — <<<Auditable>>>

  Each governance infers its extension type from the parameter of the method annotated
  with {{{/apidocs/net/officefloor/plugin/governance/clazz/Govern.html}@Govern}}.  Any
  managed object implementing that type in a governed function is enrolled automatically
  via <<<OfficeGovernance.enableAutoWireExtensions()>>>.

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestgovernance/Auditable.java|ignoreDownloadError=false}



*The governed managed object — <<<AuditRecord>>>

  <<<AuditRecord>>> is a PROCESS-scoped managed object declared in
  <<<officefloor/managedobjects/>>>.  It implements <<<Auditable>>>, which is what makes
  it eligible for governance enrollment.  Because the scope is PROCESS, each request gets
  a fresh instance with an empty event list — the perfect scratchpad for tracking what
  happened within that request.

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestgovernance/AuditRecord.java|ignoreDownloadError=false}

%{snippet|file=${basedir}/src/main/resources/officefloor/managedobjects/AuditRecord.yml|ignoreDownloadError=false}



*The governance — <<<AuditGovernance>>>

  A class-based governance uses three annotations from the
  <<<net.officefloor.plugin.governance.clazz>>> package:

   * {{{/apidocs/net/officefloor/plugin/governance/clazz/Govern.html}@Govern}} — called
     by OfficeFloor <<before>> the function body executes, once per enrolled managed
     object.  The parameter type declares the extension interface.  Analogous to beginning
     a transaction.

   * {{{/apidocs/net/officefloor/plugin/governance/clazz/Enforce.html}@Enforce}} — called
     by OfficeFloor <<after>> the function body completes successfully.  Analogous to
     committing a transaction.

   * {{{/apidocs/net/officefloor/plugin/governance/clazz/Disregard.html}@Disregard}} —
     called by OfficeFloor when the function throws an unhandled exception.  Analogous to
     rolling back a transaction.

  A new instance of the governance class is created per function invocation, so storing
  the enrolled object as a field is safe and shared across all three lifecycle methods:

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestgovernance/AuditGovernance.java|ignoreDownloadError=false}

  For more complex governance — declaring flow outputs, injecting dependencies, or
  performing asynchronous setup — extend
  {{{/apidocs/net/officefloor/compile/spi/governance/source/impl/AbstractGovernanceSource.html}AbstractGovernanceSource}}
  and reference the class with <<<source:>>> in the YAML instead of <<<class:>>>.



*Configuring governance — <<<officefloor/govern/>>>

  Governance is declared as YAML files in <<<src/main/resources/officefloor/govern/>>>.
  The file name becomes the governance name used in <<<govern:>>> lists.  The <<<class>>>
  field names the governance implementation:

%{snippet|file=${basedir}/src/main/resources/officefloor/govern/audit.yml|ignoreDownloadError=false}

  Any number of YAML files may be placed in <<<officefloor/govern/>>>; each declares one
  governance.  A <<<source:>>> field accepts a custom
  {{{/apidocs/net/officefloor/compile/spi/governance/source/impl/AbstractGovernanceSource.html}AbstractGovernanceSource}}
  implementation for cases that require flow outputs or properties:

+--
governance:
  source: com.example.MyGovernanceSource
  properties:
    timeout.ms: 5000
+--



